# React Native Image Crop — Always Use ViewShot Viewport Capture

## Rule

When implementing image cropping in a React Native / Expo app, always use
`react-native-view-shot` to capture the visible crop zone. Never implement
coordinate math to reverse-map display transforms back to native pixel space.

## Why

Coordinate math approaches (computing originX/originY from pan/scale transforms)
consistently produce wrong crops because:

- React Native transform order (scale vs translate) creates coordinate space
  ambiguity that is hard to reason about
- Device pixel ratio, image display size vs native size, and canvas centering
  all compound the error
- Different Android devices and Expo SDK versions behave differently

The ViewShot approach is WYSIWYG by definition — it captures exactly what the
user sees, with zero math.

## Required implementation pattern

### 1. Install the dependency

```bash
npx expo install react-native-view-shot
```

### 2. Render the image twice

- Once in the main canvas (visible to user, handles gestures)
- Once inside a `<ViewShot>` container positioned off-screen at `top: -9999, left: -9999`

The ViewShot image uses the same `transform` state as the visible image,
offset by the crop zone position so it renders the correct region.

### 3. ViewShot container must be off-screen, not opacity 0

```jsx
// CORRECT — off-screen, invisible, still renderable and capturable

  


// WRONG — opacity 0 blocks the visible circle underneath on Android

```

### 4. On save: capture then resize only, no crop step

```js
// Capture the viewport
const capturedUri = await viewShotRef.current.capture();

// Resize to output size and compress — no crop action needed
const result = await ImageManipulator.manipulateAsync(
  capturedUri,
  [{ resize: { width: OUTPUT_SIZE, height: OUTPUT_SIZE } }],
  { compress: quality, format: ImageManipulator.SaveFormat.JPEG }
);
```

### 5. Never use ImageManipulator crop with computed coordinates

```js
// NEVER DO THIS for WYSIWYG crop
await ImageManipulator.manipulateAsync(imageUri, [
  { crop: { originX, originY, width, height } }, // coordinate math always drifts
]);

// ALWAYS DO THIS instead
const capturedUri = await viewShotRef.current.capture(); // what you see = what you get
await ImageManipulator.manipulateAsync(capturedUri, [{ resize: ... }]);
```

## Overlay pattern for circular crop UI

Use 4 black rectangles around the circle plus a thick border ring for corners.
Do not use a single view with borderRadius as a mask over siblings — it does
not clip sibling views on Android.

```jsx
{/* Top, Bottom, Left, Right black strips */}





{/* Corner mask: thick black ring fills square corners the rectangles miss */}


{/* White circle border visible to user */}

```

## File size constraint

After capture and resize, compress iteratively until under the size budget:

```js
const qualities = [0.85, 0.7, 0.55, 0.4, 0.25, 0.1];
for (const q of qualities) {
  result = await ImageManipulator.manipulateAsync(
    capturedUri,
    [{ resize: { width: 500, height: 500 } }],
    { compress: q, format: ImageManipulator.SaveFormat.JPEG }
  );
  const buf = await (await fetch(result.uri)).arrayBuffer();
  if (buf.byteLength <= MAX_BYTES) break;
}
```
